The characteristic equation of a closed-loop system is given by setting the denominator of the closed-loop transfer function to zero. This is expressed as: 

\begin{align*} 1 + G(s)H(s) &= 0 \\ \text{where } G(s) & \text{ is the forward transfer function and } H(s) \text{ is the feedback transfer function.} \\ \text{Thus, the characteristic equation is } 1 + G(s)H(s) &= 0. \\ \text{This equation is used to determine the stability of the system.} \\ \text{Therefore, the characteristic equation is } 1 + G(s)H(s) &= 0. 
\end{align*}

The radio horizon for each tower is given by the formula: 

d = 4.1 \times \text{sqrt}(h) \text{ km}

For each tower, with height h = 100 m:

d = 4.1 \times \text{sqrt}(100) = 4.1 \times 10 = 41 \text{ km}

The maximum separation is the sum of the horizons of both towers:

d_{max} = 41 \text{ km} + 41 \text{ km} = 82 \text{ km}.
